  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given a string SSS of length NNN, containing only the characters `a`, `b`, and `c`.\n\nIn one move, you can modify SSS as follows:\n\n\nChoose three indices i,j,ki, j, ki,j,k (1≤i&lt;j&lt;k≤∣S∣1 \\leq i \\lt j \\lt k \\leq |S|1≤i&lt;j&lt;k≤∣S∣) such that Si=a,Sj=b,Sk=cS_i = \\texttt{a}, S_j = \\texttt{b}, S_k = \\texttt{c}Si​=a,Sj​=b,Sk​=c.\n\nThat is, choose some subsequence of SSS that equals `abc`.\nThen, delete either the `a` or the `c` from SSS, that is, either index iii or index kkk.\n\nNote that this reduces the length of SSS by 111.\nFor example, if S=cbba‾cb‾ac‾cS = \\texttt{cbb\\underline{a}c\\underline{b}a\\underline{c}c}S=cbba​cb​ac​c, you can choose i=4,j=6,k=8i = 4, j = 6, k = 8i=4,j=6,k=8 (the underlined indices), and then delete either index 444 (obtaining S=cbbcbaccS = \\texttt{cbbcbacc}S=cbbcbacc) or index 888 (obtaining S=cbbacbacS = \\texttt{cbbacbac}S=cbbacbac).\n\nFind the **minimum** number of moves that can be made on SSS, such that it's impossible to perform any further moves on the resulting string.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the length of SSS.\nThe second line of each test case contains the string SSS of length NNN.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line minimum possible number of moves that can be made on SSS, such that it's not possible to perform any further moves.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤3⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 3\\cdot 10^51≤N≤3⋅105",
      "Si∈{’a’,&nbsp;’b’,&nbsp;’c’}S_i \\in \\{\\texttt{'a', 'b', 'c'}\\}Si​∈{’a’,&nbsp;’b’,&nbsp;’c’}",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 3⋅1053\\cdot 10^53⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n4\nabcc\n9\ncbcaacacb\n7\naabacbc",
        "output": "1\n0\n2",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Choose i=1,j=2,k=3i = 1, j = 2, k = 3i=1,j=2,k=3 and delete index 111. Now, S=bccS = \\texttt{bcc}S=bcc and no more moves can be performed.\nTest case 222: No moves can be performed for the given string, so the answer is 000.\nTest case 333: One optimal solution is as follows:\n\nChoose i=2,j=6,k=7i = 2, j = 6, k = 7i=2,j=6,k=7, and delete index 777.\nNow, S=aabacbS = \\texttt{aabacb}S=aabacb.\nChoose i=2,j=3,k=5i = 2, j = 3, k = 5i=2,j=3,k=5, and delete index 555.\nNow, S=aababS = \\texttt{aabab}S=aabab, and no more moves can be performed.\n\nIt can be shown that using less than 222 moves is not possible."
      }
    ]
